## 1. INTRODUCTION
Recently the multifunctionalization of microporous metal− organic frameworks (MMOFs) has become one of the principal focuses of inorganic and materials chemistry because multiple physical properties may coexist and even synergetically interact.1 Therefore, better performance or tuning of functions or new applications is expected.1,2 In this context, magnetic MMOFs especially those displaying long-range ordering are viewed as one of the significant candidates of multifunctional molecular materials.3 In order to achieve long-range ordered magnetic materials one of the first requirement is the consideration of distance between the nearest-neighbors moment carriers.4 In general, the critical temperature decreases exponentially with increasing the number of nonmagnetic atoms bridging the carriers. For example, metals have Curie temperature $(\dot{T}_{\mathrm{C}})$ approaching $1400~\mathrm{K},$ oxides have high $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ up to nearly $900~\mathrm{K},$ while for cyanides, the $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ is below $350\mathrm{K},$ and those containing three-atom bridges are well below $50~\mathrm{K}^{4}$ As such, one of us previously made the statement that long-range magnetic ordering and porosity are inimical based on the idea that long-range magnetic ordering requires a short connection between moment carriers and that porosity requires long ones.5 This has been shown not to be the case under the condition that there are fragments containing $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{O}-\mathbf{M}$ connections within the framework and weak magnetic interaction exists between them.3,6 Thus, it is possible to classify the following four types of magnetic MMOFs: (i) organic molecules act as linkers between 1D inorganic $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ chains, so-called rodspacer; (ii) organic pillars between inorganic $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ layers, so-called pillared-layer; (iii) organic molecules sitting in the gallery of inorganic $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ layers; and (iv) organic ligands connecting discrete $\mathbf{M-O-M}$ clusters, containing two or more metal ions or isolated metal ions, so-called cluster base MMOFs. The same analogy can be done for cyanides, but their transition temperatures for layer and chain compounds are expected to be lower than those with $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ connections.3c
Among the rod-spacer compounds of type I the magnetic ground states of $\mathrm{Co_{3}(O H)_{2}(C_{4}\mathrm{\bar{O}_{4})_{2}\cdot3H_{2}O^{7}}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}^{-}(1,4-$ chdc) $_2(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{8}$ were found to be modified by the presence of the lattice water. In the presence of water both are antiferromagnet, while in the absence the former is a ferromagnet and the latter is a ferrimagnet. Presumably the electron density of the solvent molecules introduces a weak antiferromagnetic exchange between the magnetic chains. Thus, the magnetic properties of these two compounds and also others established the importance of the solvents in magnetic MMOFs in contributing to the exchange.9 Therefore, we may anticipate that if the chains are connected by longer nonmagnetic ligands and in the absence of solvent in the cavities, the chains may behave as single-chain magnets, while in the presence of solvent they may exhibit long-range orderin g.6,10 Using the same arguments, those of type IV would be single molecule magnet.11 Many more examples showing modulation of $T_{\mathrm{C}},\ T_{\mathrm{N}},$ or magnetic hysteresis have since been reported but only few show changes of ground state and usually two at most,3,6−8,12 which was mainly due to the removal/inclusion/exchange of guest molecules (Table S1). In porous 3D framework, such transformation from an antiferromagnet to ferromagnet, ferrimagnet even single-chain magnet, single molecule magnet is rarely observed.13 On the other hand, in rod-spacer MMOFs, the introduction of longer ligand is possible and beneficial for generating large voids. Hence, we expect magnetic MMOFs with rod-spacer feature to be a typical model to investigate magnetic switching behavior and also a good candidate of multifunctional molecular materials.
In order to test the above hypothesis, we made the cobalt analogue $[\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}_{3}(\mathrm{{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{{pybz})_{2}]}}$ ·3DMF (1·3DMF, purple) of the only double-walled framework, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}]{\cdot2.5\mathrm{DMF}},$ which has been shown to contain $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ connectivity within the well-separated square chains.14 Solvent exchanged $[\mathbf{Co}_{\ 3}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}^{\mathrm{{-}}}]$ ·xSolvent $(x S\mathrm{olvent\=\6MeOH,\}$ purple; $4.5\mathrm{EtOH},$ , purple; $3\mathrm{PrOH},$ purple; $2C_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6},$ purple), iodine loaded $[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{\bar{II}}}{}_{3}(\mathrm{\bar{lac})}_{2}(\mathrm{pybz)}_{2}]{\cdot2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ $\left(\mathbf{1}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2},\right.$ black), and desolvated $[\mathbf{Co}_{\ 3}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}]$ (1, purple) were obtained in SC-SC manner and the magnetic behaviors were investigated to reveal the relevance between host−guest interaction and magnetic ground state. Furthermore, given that post-synthetic modification (PSM) can modify either the organic linker or the coordination of the metal center without perturbing the parent framework and modify its physical properties,15,16 we successfully conducted PSM on 1 by coordination and oxidation of the metal center to obtain two derivatives, hydrated $\mathrm{\Gamma[{Co}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{pybz)}_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ $\left({\bf{1}}{\bf{a}}^{\cdot}7\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}\right)$ and partially oxidized $\big[\mathrm{Co^{III}C o^{I I}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{pybz}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{lac}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}\big]\mathrm{I}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}$ 1.5DMSO $\left(\mathbf{16}{\cdot}\mathrm{I}^{-}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{1}{\cdot}5\mathrm{D}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{O}\right)$ . Here, we report the syntheses, structures, interconversions, Raman spectra, thermal stability, gas sorption, and magnetic properties of these compounds (Scheme 1). As-prepared 1·3DMF, solvent exchanged 1·xSolvent, and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ loaded $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}$ behave as canted antiferromagnets, while upon desolvation 1 behaves as a singlechain magnet, and 1 is transformed into a ferrimagnet when it is hydrated $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . By partial oxidation of 1 or $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{DMSO/H_{2}O}$ ferromagnetic $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}}\mathbf{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}{\cdot}1.5\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}$ is obtained. These changes were possible by the PSM of the asprepared 1·3DMF which alters the magnetic exchange interaction between the chains of cobalt ions from being antiferromagnetic through the electron density of the guests in the channel, to being nonexistent in the desolvated 1, and finally to ferromagnetic through $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and DMSO. The series of compound realize for the first time the switching between four ground states by the modulation of host−guest interaction, magnetic $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ chain, and also the oxidation state of the spin carrier.
