## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
3.1. Crystal Structures. The key feature of the five structures is the square pillars consisting of octahedral and tetrahedral cobalt ions and a racemic mixture of lactate ions $(\mathrm{lac}^{2-})$ connected through the long pybz forming double walls between the pillars (Figure 1). The structure is the same as that for the $Z\mathrm{n}$ analogue but 1·3DMF crystallizes in the space group $P\mathrm{-}4c_{2}$ instead of $P{-}42_{1}c$ for the $Z\mathrm{n}$ analogue. It contains two crystallographically distinct cobalt atoms in the ratio 2 (Co1, distorted octahedral):1 (Co2, tetrahedral) (Figure S1). Co1 is located on a general position and has a distorted octahedral geometry with five oxygen atoms $\left[\mathrm{Co-O}=1.988-2.334~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right]$ of two lac and one pybz and one nitrogen atom $[\mathrm{Co-N}=2.105$ Å] of another pybz (Figure S2). Co2 is located at the $4e$ position and adopts a tetrahedral geometry with two deprotonated hydroxyl groups $[\mathrm{Co-O}\ \stackrel{\sim}{=}\ 1.905\ \mathrm{\AA}]$ of two lac and two oxygen atom $\big[\mathrm{Co-O}=1.963~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\big]$ of carboxylate from two pybz. The lac exhibits $\eta_{5},\mu_{2},\mu_{2},\mu_{1}$ -coordination modes, and the equivalent lac anions alternately link ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ into the square tubular $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{(D-/L-lac)}_{2}]^{2+}$ chains parallel to the $c$ -axis.
This chain can be viewed as a square with vertex of four equivalent six-coordinated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions $\mathbf{\zeta}^{\prime}\mathbf{Col{\cdots}}\mathbf{ColA}$ distance of 4.391 Å) bridged by a single oxygen atom of a lac $({\mathrm{Col-O4-}}$ Co1 of $162.9^{\circ}\rangle$ linked by two four-coordinated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{Col}\cdots\mathbf{Co}2$ distance of $3.4\dot{2}4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) by two bridges of one oxygen atom and a syn−syn carboxylate of pybz $(\mathrm{Co1-O3-Co2}$ of $123.1^{\circ})$ (Table 1, Table S4). The Co1−O4−Co1 angles for 1· 3DMF, $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}6\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , 1, $\mathrm{\mathbf{1\cdot2C_{6}H}}_{6},$ and $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}3\mathrm{PrOH}$ are $163.0^{\circ}$ ,
$162.6^{\circ}$ , $164.3^{\circ}$ , $162.5^{\circ}$ , and $162.8^{\circ}$ , respectively. It should be noted that the structure differs from previous linear, zigzag, ladder, helical, and ribbon chains widely found in 1D, 2D, or 3D coordination polymers which use a single metal center as node.3d The present thick and rigid chain can be viewed as constructed by their two square alternately connected vertexes (Figure 1). Such a feature renders both the chain and the framework high stability that is crucial for subsequent PSM.15 Especially, the dominant $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ connectivity in the chain is crucial for magnetism, hence variation of such connectivity may greatly affect the magnetic behavior of the compound.6
These chains of cobalt are further linked by double pybz bridges along the $a$ and $b$ directions, forming a 3D neutral framework with square 1D channels which are occupied by guest solvent in 1·xSolvent and empty in 1. Taking the van der Waals radii of the atoms into consideration, using PLATON the window of these channels was estimated to be ca. $10.8\times$ $10.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ (distance of pairs of the ligand constituted the opposite walls) with a solvent accessible volume of $43.1\%$ of the unit cell.20 The framework is retained for all five of the compounds except for the small variation of bond distances and angles. The very low residual electron density in the crystal structure analyses of 1 indicates that all of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ has been removed from the channels. No global change in connectivity occurs in the framework upon desolvation except for minimal changes in metal−ligand bond distances and angles. The average coordination bond lengths of Co1 and $\mathbf{Co}2$ in 1 are 2.120 and $1.943\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively, which are slightly longer than those [2.111 and 1.934 Å] of 1·3DMF. This difference may be due to the higher temperature at which the data of 1 was collected. The solvents, 3DMF, 4.5EtOH, $\begin{array}{r}{6\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH},}\end{array}$ $3\mathrm{PrOH}_{\cdot}$ , and $2\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ per molecular unit within the channels, are reasonable and quite compact even though most of them were refined in disorder models.
3.2. Thermal Properties. Thermogravimetric analyses of all the compounds show the destruction of the framework at ca. $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Before being destroyed it is stable for a wide temperature range over $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S3, Table S5). Such thermal stability is comparable to the highly thermo-stable $\mathrm{MMOFs}^{21}$ and can be associated with the strong intra- and inter-rod connection that also contributes to subsequent PSM. The solvents are removed slowly from room temperature in all cases, but the temperature at which all the solvents are removed differs from compound to compound. This temperature is an indication of the strength of the interaction between the solvent molecules and the solvent with the framework, and it increases in the order methanol $\angle$ ethanol $\prec$ propanol $\angle$ benzene $\mathbf{\Lambda}<\mathrm{DMF}$ . Except for the anomalous temperature for benzene, the order respects the boiling point of the liquids, which suggests the solvent−solvent interaction cannot be ignored.
When the crystals of 1 are immersed in water or exposed to water vapor, they turn green. TGA of the green crystals 1a· $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ exhibits gradual loss of water from room temperature with a step at ca. $165^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(13.3\%)$ and finishes at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\left(3.9\%\right)$ (Figure S4). The first loss amounts to $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (calculated $13.8\%$ ) and the second to $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (calculated $4.0\%$ ) (Table S5). We infer that $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ resides in the channels, and the other two are coordinated to the cobalt. The coordinated water to the highly distorted octahedral cobalt Co1 will force it to become more regular, and the color of that center is expected to be pink. Consequently, in the presence of the blue tetrahedral cobalt the overall color of the compound will appear green.22
3.3. Powder $\mathsf{\pmb X}$ -ray Diffraction. PXRD has been used to check the purity of the samples and also to study the structural changes that take place during certain modification. First the experimental PXRD of 1·3DMF is the same as that calculated using the single crystal result (Figure S4). After desolvating, the PXRD of 1 is again similar to that calculated using the single crystal result. The temperature dependence of the transformation of 1·3DMF to 1 was studied in situ, and it is found to be a smooth one with retention of the crystallinity. However, when 1 is soaked in water or exposed to water vapor it is transformed to a poorly crystalline material $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure S5). The crystallinity is further deteriorated upon dehydrating of $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}^{\mathrm{Ii}}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}]$ $\left({\bf{1}}^{\prime}\right)$ . The XRD pattern for the latter appears to be very different to the original 1. From the patterns no structural characteristics can be elucidated.
3.4. Raman and IR Spectroscopy. The Raman spectra (Figure 2) of 1·3DMF, 1, $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , and $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5}\mathrm{DMSO}$ belong to two different classes, that is those without water in the crystals (1·3DMF and 1) and those with $\mathrm{(1a^{.7}H_{2}O}$ and 1b· $\mathrm{I}^{-}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}1.5\mathrm{D}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{O})$ . The bond energies and the differences between them for the two classes can be used to derive the coordination geometries of the metals and the modes of bonding of the ligands.23 The Raman bands, which are not perturbed by the presence of water, are assigned to the organic ligand pybz. In contrast, the bands which are perturbed are assigned to the lactate ions and the metal−ligand vibrations. Our assumption, based on our previous work24 and the crystallographic data reported by Bourne et al.,25 is that the long bond and the strained coordination of the carboxylate of the lactate to Co1 are easily removed by the coordination of incoming water to give a regular octahedral Co1. The $\nu(\mathrm{OH})$ band of water for $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5}\mathrm{DMSO}$ is at $3216~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The region of the carboxylate vibrations $(1100-$ $1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) has four intense bands which can be identified by the difference between carboxylate $\nu_{\mathrm{{s}}}(\mathrm{{OCO})}$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathrm{OCO})$ .23 The difference of ca. $146~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the four compounds is consistent with that of a relaxed carboxylate, and thus we assigned these pairs of bands to the benzoate of pybz. In contrast for the highly strained lactate the difference is 188 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for 1·3DMF and 1, while it is reduced to $136~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ upon solvation with water. Thus it is reasonable to assume that the long ${\mathrm{Co-O}}(\mathrm{lac})$ bond is broken while relieving the strain of the coordination.24,25 This is associated to the color change from purple to green consistent with the change of electronic structure resulting in a lower extinction coefficient for the less distorted octahedral cobalt, which in the presence of the expected blue color of the tetrahedral cobalt will give a jade green color.22 The bending modes lowering from 844 (1· 3DMF and 1) to $830~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{(1a^{.7}H_{2}O}$ and $\mathbf{16{\cdot}I^{-}{\cdot}}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}$ 1.5DMSO) are again in line with the arguments presented above. For 1·3DMF and 1, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co-O}}$ stretching modes are numerous in the range $350{-}450~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which is due to the highly irregular octahedron around Co1 and they collapse into one band at $308~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{16{\cdot}I}^{-}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}$ 1.5DMSO.24,25 As a comparison, the IR spectra of these four compounds were dominated by carboxyl groups and water guest molecules and also can be divided to two different classes like the Raman spectra. And the similar information further confirmed the structure transformations and the stability of the host framework (Figure S6). In the absence of crystal structures for $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5D M S O}_{;}$ , the Raman and IR spectra were useful in elucidating the molecular structures and thus allow further discussion of structure−magnetic properties relationship.
3.5. Gas Sorption. The observation of a stable porous framework with large channels of $10.8\times10.8\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ after removal of the solvents prompted us to study the gas sorption properties especially in view of the presence of a surface having different potential sites for sorption. We have therefore study the sorption of gases $(\Nu_{2},\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ up to 1 bar and $\mathrm{H}_{2},\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ up to 40 bar) and solvent vapors $\mathrm{(C_{6}H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ up to 1 bar) for 1 and $\mathbf{{1}^{\prime}}$ (desolvated $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\dot{}.$ ), respectively.
The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm for 1 at 77 K shows a typical type I behavior with maximum uptake of $256~\mathsf{c m}^{3}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (STP) at 1 bar (Figures 3 and S7), corresponding to a Langmuir surface area of $\overline{{1050~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}}}$ , which is comparable to the Zn analog and among one of the highest for magnetic MMOFs (Table S6). $^{14,26}$ It demonstrates once again that the rod-spacer strategy is an effective method to construct magnetic MMOFs with large voids by the introduction of longer linker. In addition, 1 shows maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $252~\mathsf{c m}^{3}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (77 K, 40 bar) and $84.8~\mathsf{c m}^{3}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (298 K, 39 bar), respectively (Figures 3 and S8). In contrast, $\mathbf{{1^{\prime}}}$ has nearly no ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption. For highpressure $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption, a similar phenomenon was also observed with a maximum uptake of $42~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (one-sixth of that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for 1) and $11.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (one-eighth of that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ for 1). Such results suggest that the 1D porous channel and/or the windows may shrink due to twist of the pybz linker during the transformation from 1 to $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ driven by the change of the coordination of one of the cobalt ions.24,27
For solvent vapor, 1 shows typical type I benzene sorption curve with saturated uptake of $249~\mathrm{\stackrel{..}{m g}~g^{-1}}$ (2.3 benzene molecule per formula unit) (Figure 3). Though sharing analogous sorption isotherm, the saturated benzene uptake of desolvated $\mathbf{{1^{\prime}}}$ ( $\cdot124\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}.$ , about 1.2 benzene molecule per formula unit) is just one-half of the value of 1. Comparing to benzene, the sorption of MeOH increases slowly with the increase of vapor pressure and the maximum uptake was 172 $\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ (4.3 methanol molecule per formula unit) at $P/P_{0}=$ 0.88. This not fully saturated value compared to the methanol exchanged crystals $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}6\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ . Hence, methanol shows recovery ability of the shrinking channels of $\mathbf{{1^{\prime}}}$ .
3.6. Enrichment versus Oxidation of 1 by $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ . In contrast to iodine uptake by the $Z\mathrm{n}$ analogue, the cobalt compound can uptake iodine as well as get oxidized. When crystals of 1 (100 mg) were soaked in aprotic cyclohexane solution of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ ( $3\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.1\mathbf{M},$ , the dark-violet coloration of the $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ solution slowly fades to transparent light purple, and the resulting crystals become very dark purple to black (Figure 4).
The uptake is $\mathrm{~0.9~}\mathrm{~g}$ iodine per gram of 1 to give $[\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}]{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}^{\circ}$ $(\mathbf{1}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2},$ iodine content of $47.8\%$ which is close to that of the $Z\mathbf{n}$ analogue $\begin{array}{r}{\big([\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}]\cdot}\end{array}$ $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ (iodine content of $49.8\%$ ). The loading process appears to be slower for 1 (Figures S9 and S10).14 The reverse process, that is $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ release was monitored by UV−vis transmission spectroscopy by soaking some crystals of $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in ethanol (Figure S11). The iodine absorption bands were found to increase slowly with time and tend toward saturation after $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Two-probe electrical conductivity measurement on a powder sample, contacted with gold paint, shows an ohmic behavior in the voltage of $^{-1}$ to $1\mathrm{V}$ (Figure S12). The conductivity $(\sigma)$ is 7 $\times10^{-6}\dot{S/\mathrm{cm}}$ for $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}$ which is higher than the $10^{-6}$ to $10^{-8}$ $\mathrm{{}}S/\mathrm{{m}}$ found for the iodine-loaded single crystal of $\mathrm{tris}\big(o-$ phenylenedioxy) cyclotriphosphazene28 but much less than that of the single crystal of the iodine-loaded $Z\mathrm{n}$ analogue of $3\times$ $10^{-3}\left(\sigma_{\parallel}\right)$ and $\mathrm{i}\times10^{-4}(\sigma_{\perp})\mathrm{S/cm}.$ 14
However, when crystals of 1 were soaked in a $\mathrm{DMSO/H_{2}O}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}=1:1\right),}\end{array}$ solution of iodine, the crystals slowly change color from purple to orange $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5}\mathrm{DMSO}$ (Figure 4). Its PXRD shows very few peaks, which is common to that found for $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and indicates that the two compounds are next to being amorphous (Figure S5). TGA shows two weight losses before the framework is destroyed at ca. $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ the first at ca. $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ amounts to $15.6\%$ which corresponds to $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 1.5DMSO solvent molecules (Figure S3, Table S5). The elemental analysis results also coincide well with such a composition. The Raman spectrum shows a close similarity in band energies as that of $\mathbf{1a}{\cdot}\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure 2). Using similar arguments as above, we can assume that the lactate is now monodentate and that one water molecule is coordinated to each Co1. Since $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5}\mathrm{DMSO}$ is not green but orange is a strong indication that the tetrahedral Co2 is no longer four coordinated but five or six. Thus, we propose that it is the Co2 that is oxidized favorably.29 Therefore, only the Co1 will be moment carriers, and a reduction in moment is found as expected (see below).
3.7. Magnetic Properties. The magnetic properties of the compounds have been studied as a function of temperature, field, and ac frequencies (Figure 5). The results can be presented by the behavior at high and low temperatures. In high-temperature regions all the compounds behave as Curie− Weiss paramagnets with differing exchange interactions.
Considering the structural parameters, interatomic distances and angles (Table 1), we can define two principal exchange interactions within each chain where both are mediated through one single oxygen atom, that is $J_{1}$ $\left(\mathrm{Col-Col}\right)$ with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co\cdots}\mathbf{Co}$ distance of 4.37−4.47 Å and $\mathsf{C o l-O-C o l}$ angle of 162.5−164.3 and $J_{2}$ $\left(\mathbf{Col-Co}2\right)$ with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co\cdots}\mathbf{Co}$ of $3.41-\Bar{3.}43\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $\mathrm{Co1-O-Co2}$ of 122.4−123.6.21,30 From the interatomic distances we can intuitively assume that $|J_{1}|<|J_{2}|$ . Secondary exchange interactions $\left(J_{3}\right)$ exist for example $_{\mathrm{Col-Co2}}$ at distances of about 6.09 Å and $_{\mathrm{Col-Col}}$ at ${\bar{6.07}}{\bar{\mathrm{A}}}.$ . The latter will be weak in comparison to the former ones. Between chains we can consider two interactions: $J_{4}$ through the connecting pybz bridge and dipolar through space and the other $(J^{\prime})$ through the solvents in the channels. The lack of magnetic ordering in 1 would suggest that $J_{4}$ is negligible.
In the case of 1·xSolvent and 1, the susceptibility in the hightemperature region can be interpreted as dominated by the strong antiferromagnetic coupling $J2$ . As $k T$ approaches ${\approx}65\mathrm{~K~}$ the Co1−Co1 antiferromagnetic coupling, $J_{1},$ becomes operative and further decrease the susceptibility. For 1· xSolvent, the presence of solvents provides enough electron density to generate the weak interchain $\left(J^{\prime}\right)$ interaction (Figure 6), and long-range magnetic ordering is observed at the same temperature of $18\pm1$ K. The strong field dependence and the weak isothermal magnetization at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ indicate the ground state for these compounds is canted antiferromagnetic.30 This is further confirmed by the low magnetization in high field. Using the magnetization $\dot{(16\mathrm{~cm}^{3}\mathrm{~G/mol})}$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ measured in 1 Oe field cooling for 1·3DMF and an expected saturation magnetization for a ferromagnetic chain of $3~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ ( $3\times5585$ $\mathrm{cm}^{\mathrm{3}}\ \mathrm{G/mol})$ , the canting angle is estimated as $0.006^{\circ}$ . For 1· $2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}$ the high-temperature behavior is almost the same, but the canting appears at lower temperature of $10\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S18) and ac susceptibilities independent of frequency of the ac field.
The high-temperature susceptibility behavior of 1 is similar to those of 1·xSolvent (Figure 5), which indicate the interactions involved are of the same signs and magnitudes. However, the low-temperature behavior is quite different. There is no long-range order, and the magnetization increases continuously to the lowest temperature of $2\ \mathrm{~K~}.$ The ac susceptibilities of 1 in a 5 Oe field oscillating at different frequencies and a zero dc field exhibit strong frequency dependence. Both the in-phase $\left(\chi^{\prime}\right)$ and out-of-phase $(\chi^{\prime\prime})$ susceptibilities peak temperatures shift with increasing frequency, for example, ${T_{\mathrm{max}}}\:\bar{(\chi^{\prime})}$ shifts from $2.7\mathrm{~K~}$ at $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ to $4.5\mathrm{~K~}$ at $997~\mathrm{Hz})$ . The relaxation process is defined by the parameter $\phi~=~(\Delta T_{\mathrm{max}}/T)/\Delta(\log{\bar{~}}\upsilon)~=~0.12,$ which is in agreement with that expected for superparamagnetism. The strong frequency-dependent behavior can be further confirmed by the Cole−Cole diagram at $3.5\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S19), which follows a generalized Debye model with $\alpha=0.4$ $\mathit{\check{R}}=2.09\times10^{-5},$ ), indicating a relative narrow distribution of relaxation times. The relaxation time $(\tau)$ of 1 follows the Arrhenius behavior ${\mathit{\Omega}}(\tau=\tau_{0}$ $\exp(\Delta_{\tau}/k_{\mathrm{B}}T)\big)$ , with $\Delta_{\tau}=41(8)\ \mathrm{K}$ and $\tau_{0}=1.7\times10^{-8}:$ s $R=$ 0.999). Therefore, with these parameters we can imagine the chains are magnetically isolated and can be considered as single-chain magnets within a 3D framework.31,32
The high-temperature behavior of the susceptibility of 1a· $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ belongs to the second class mentioned above. It does not show the broad maximum at $65\mathrm{K}$ and instead the susceptibility increases on cooling below $120\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 5). This indicates that $J_{1}$ is now ferromagnetic (Figure 6). To discuss the magnetic properties we have to recall that the Raman spectroscopy has indicated a change of geometry of Co1 from a highly distorted to a regular octahedron. Thus, this structural change has major effect on the susceptibility due to the change of the electronic structure and especially with the additional anisotropy of the Co1 in the regular octahedral geometry. The low Weiss temperature suggests the modification of the geometry of Co1 induces either a reduction in both $J_{1}$ and $J_{2}$ .
The $\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ versus $T$ plot is characteristic of that of a ferrimagnet with a minimum at 100 K. The ZFC-FC magnetization in a field of $10\mathrm{~\Oe~}$ exhibits a bifurcation temperature of $32\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S20). Both component of the ac susceptibilities are nonzero, and $\chi^{\prime}$ has a peak at $27.0\mathrm{K}$ at $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ which is shifted to $28.5\mathrm{K}$ at $1\mathrm{kHz}$ . $\chi^{\prime\prime}$ becomes nonzero below $33\ \mathrm{K},$ confirming a critical temperature of $32\pm1~\mathrm{K},$ but the frequency dependence is weaker. The magnetization below 33 K is field dependent due to the long-range ordering. The isothermal magnetization at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ is hysteretic with a coercive field of $2.4\mathrm{kOe}$ , saturation magnetization at $50\mathrm{kOe}$ of $5.2\ \mu_{\mathrm{{B}}},$ and a remanant magnetization of $0.84~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ (Figures 7 and S20).
Both the coercive field and the remanant magnetization decrease upon warming. All the characteristics, except for the saturation magnetization of $5.2\mu_{\mathrm{B}},$ tend to the conclusion of a ferrimagnetic ground state.33
If the exchange interactions, $J_{1}$ and $J_{2},$ are antiferromagnetic we can have a situation as depicted in Figure 7, where the moments of two Co1 are antiparallel to the other two Co1 and to the two Co2. Assuming the moments of each chain are oriented equally in the same direction, then a ferrimagnet results but the saturation moment is expected to be $3\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ (that is $+1\times2.4~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ per Co1, $-1\times2.4~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ per Co1, $+1\times3~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ per Co2). To account for a saturation of $5.2\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}},$ we may invoke alignment of the moments of Co1 and Co2 along different axes given the different anisotropies. Thus, we propose one possible solution where the four Co1 moments, behaving as xy, point away from the center of the square in the $a b$ -plane but with a canting along the $c$ -axis and the Co2 moments, behaving as Ising, lie along the $c$ -axis. The surplus moment requires that each Co1 moment be canted by $\sin^{-1}(1.1/2)=3.66^{\circ}$ from the $a b$ -plane. Such a model requires neutron diffraction to confirm its validity, but such an experiment is not feasible due to the amorphous nature of the compound.
For $\mathbf{16^{\bullet}I^{-}{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}1.5}\mathrm{DMSO},\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ is only $6.48~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}~\mathrm{K}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 5). On lowering temperature it is decreased to $5.22~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{\mol^{-1}~K}$ at $30~\mathrm{K}$ before reaching a maximum of 16.11 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\mathrm{K}$ at $7.0\mathrm{~K~}$ The Curie−Weiss fit of the hightemperature data gives a negative $\theta$ of $-6(1)$ K and $C=6.91$ ${\mathrm{cm}}^{3^{\bullet}}{\mathrm{mol}}^{-1}{\mathrm{K}}.$ Both the ac susceptibilities show a peak at ca. 7 K which is independent of frequency suggesting long-range ordering. The isothermal magnetization at $2~\mathrm{~K~}$ increases gradually with field without saturation at ${\boldsymbol{5}}\operatorname{T}$ and shows a small hysteresis of $340~\mathrm{Oe}$ and remanant magnetization of $0.334~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ (Figure S21). The maximum magnetization value is $4.65~\mu_{\mathrm{B}},$ which is close to that expected for two ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ ions of $4.6~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}.$ These characteristics suggest a ferromagnetic ground state,3 which according to the discussion above consist of parallel octahedral Co1 moments. The low Curie temperature is consistent with the weaker exchange interaction between Co1 within the squares and even weaker Co1−Co1 between squares via the oxidized diamagnetic Co2.
Hence, the magnetism appears to be little affected by the different solvents as 1·3DMF and 1·xSolvent all show cantedantiferromagnetism, but their presence is important to provide weak antiferromagnetic interchain couplings to give a longrange ordered ground state (Figure 6). By removing the guests those weak couplings are turned off, and the magnetism is that of noninteracting chains, therefore a superparamagnetic state akin single-chain magnetism is observed. By modifying the geometry through coordination of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to a more regular octahedron for Co1, the strength of the $_{\mathrm{Col-Col}}$ and $\mathrm{Col-}$ Co2 is drastically reduced, and in addition anisotropy of Co1 is elevated. These structural and magnetic anisotropy changes result in a ferrimagnetic state due to a ferromagnetic interchain coupling. Upon partial oxidation of the tetrahedral Co2 site that results in it becoming diamagnetic $\left(\mathrm{d}^{6},S=0\right)$ , a ferromagnetic state is stabilized, as the interchain coupling is ferromagnetic.
## 4. CONCLUSION
$[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}]$ as its $Z\mathbf{n}$ analogue is one of the most stable MMOFs due to its very strong square rod of Co-lactate and the double wall connection between them. Single-crystal to singlecrystal transformation confirms the removal of the solvents and the porous channels can house different solvents (DMF, methanol, ethanol, propanol and benzene) and gases $(\Nu_{2},\mathrm{~H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4,\overline{{{\mathrm{\tiny~Ch}}}}}^{\mathrm{\tiny~\cdot~}}$ ) reversibly. Post-synthetic modification with water changes the geometry of the cobalt, while $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{DMSO/H_{2}O}$ partially oxidized the metal ions. Raman spectroscopy confirms these structural changes. The magnetism resulted for the first time in the observation of four magnetic ground states in any material, canted antiferromagnets for $\big[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}\big].$ xSolvent and iodine-loaded samples due to the interchain coupling $\left(J^{\prime}\right)$ via the solvent or iodine molecules being negative, single-chain magnet for the desolvated $[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}^{\circ}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}]$ as $J^{\prime}$ approached 0, ferrimagnet for $\mathrm{\small{[Co^{H}_{3}(p y b z)_{2}(l a c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}},$ · $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as $J^{\prime}>0_{;}$ , and ferromagnet for the partially oxidized $\big[\mathrm{Co^{III}C o^{I I}\bar{\Phi}_{2}(p y b z)}_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\big]\mathrm{\bar{I}\cdot2H_{2}O\cdot1.5D\bar{M}S O}$ as $J^{\prime}>0$ . In addition, the iodine-loaded $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}(\mathrm{lac})_{2}]{\cdot2.7\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ is a weak conductor. This family of compounds derived from a single framework is unique in its stability, gas sorption, and solvent exchange and especially its stabilization of four magnetic ground states as well as introducing electrical conductivity in a MMOF. It also presents a vivid example of chemically driven dynamic magnetic MMOFs. We believe that the different chemistry bringing in multifunctionalities in MMOFs will be useful in the development of emergent materials for the future.
